<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- One entitlement, and it is not a permission the app asks the user for
     up front: Apple Events consent is requested by macOS at the moment the
     scripting fallback first drives Music or Spotify, and is refusable.

     It is required rather than optional. The app is signed with the hardened
     runtime, which denies in-process Apple Events outright without this key —
     so without it `PlayerBridge`'s AppleScript route fails with -1743 in every
     signed build, which is precisely the route `MediaController` falls back to
     when the MediaRemote helper is unavailable. Every branch of the media
     failure chain would end somewhere that cannot work.

     `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription` in the Info.plist is the sentence shown in
     that prompt; it is not a substitute for this. Calendar's entitlement was
     dropped with the Calendar feature and is not coming back. -->
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- A keychain access group is added here at build time, but only for
         Developer ID builds — see `Scripts/bundle.sh`. It is what moves the
         Spotify tokens into the data-protection keychain, where they are
         scoped to the team identity rather than to the exact binary that
         wrote them, so macOS stops asking for the login password every time
         the app is re-signed. The group must carry the team prefix to be
         valid, and an ad-hoc signature has no team: claiming one anyway makes
         launchd refuse to start the app at all (spawn error 163). Ad-hoc
         builds therefore use the legacy keychain, where the developer answers
         one prompt per rebuild. See `KeychainStore`. -->
</dict>
</plist>
